AD  - University of Sharjah, Center For Data Analytics and Cybersecurity, Research Institute of Sciences and Engineering, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates
AB  - Recently, Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) have had a pivotal role in the new generation of wireless networks due to their ease of deployment, mobility, and flexibility. UAV-assisted networks enable smartening networks across various applications, including disaster response, smart cities, military, surveillance, agriculture, and more. Within these networks, UAVs play different roles in the network, such as mobile base stations, edge computing nodes, aggregation servers, and communication relays. Given the decentralized nature of UAVs, federated learning became a promising solution for training models, offering data privacy and reduced network overhead through on-device training. However, the resource-constrained and dynamic nature of the UAVs introduces different challenges. This paper presents a preliminary review of resource optimization mechanisms that optimize UAV-FL operations, including task offloading, energy efficiency, scheduling, and optimization frameworks. It explores several techniques that were proposed to balance the constrained resources of UAVs with the model performance. It also highlights the customization in FL that helps reduce delay and optimize training speed. Moreover, it presents optimization problem formulations and associated algorithms. Finally, it covers the limitations of the existing solutions and potential future directions. © 2025 IEEE.
