AB  - The rise of algorithmic surveillance in the United States and the United Arab Emirates presents profound challenges to privacy, due process, and human rights. AI-driven technologies such as predictive policing, facial recognition, and behavioral profiling are deployed under the guise of national security and crime prevention. While the United States has a constitutional and judicial framework that provides some protections for privacy, gaps persist due to decentralization and reliance on private data aggregators. In the UAE, a hybrid legal system grants the state broad discretion over surveillance, raising concerns about transparency and proportionality. This paper critically examines the regulatory, legal, and ethical implications of algorithmic surveillance in both countries, proposing a comparative framework for reconciling national security imperatives with human rights protections. © 2025 IEEE.
